You want to host a static website on S3. What are the steps?
Answer:
Create an S3 bucket (name must match the domain name, e.g., mywebsite.com).
Upload website files (HTML, CSS, JS).
Enable Static Website Hosting under bucket properties.
Set index document (e.g., index.html) and error document (e.g., error.html).
Update Bucket Policy to allow s3:GetObject for public access:

{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": "*",
  "Action": "s3:GetObject",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::mywebsite.com/*"
}
Access via the website endpoint URL provided by AWS.
Bonus: Use CloudFront + ACM SSL certificate to make it HTTPS-enabled.

3️⃣ Scenario: You need to automatically move data older than 30 days to cheaper storage. How?
Answer:
Use S3 Lifecycle Policies.
Create a rule like:
Transition to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days.
Transition to S3 Glacier after 90 days.
Permanently delete after 365 days (optional).
Configure via the AWS Console, CLI, or JSON rule in the bucket settings.

4️⃣ Scenario: Two users upload objects with the same key name at the same time. What happens?
Answer:
In S3, objects are overwritten if they have the same key name.
If Versioning is enabled, both versions are stored with unique version IDs.
If Versioning is disabled, the latest upload overwrites the previous object.
Tip: Always enable Versioning in environments with concurrent uploads.

5️⃣ Scenario: You want to restrict S3 access to only one specific IAM role or VPC.
Answer:
Use Bucket Policies with conditions.
Example 1: Restrict to specific IAM role
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/EC2ReadRole" },
  "Action": "s3:*",
  "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::mybucket", "arn:aws:s3:::mybucket/*"]
}

Example 2: Restrict to VPC Endpoint
"Condition": {
  "StringEquals": {
    "aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-1a2b3c4d"
  }
}

6️⃣ Scenario: Your application is getting “Access Denied” when accessing an S3 object. What steps do you take to troubleshoot?

Answer:
Check if Bucket Policy or Object ACL allows access.
Confirm IAM role or user policy grants s3:GetObject permission.
Verify S3 Block Public Access settings (may override policies).
If accessing from VPC, confirm VPC Endpoint policy allows it.
Check if the object is encrypted with KMS — the IAM user/role must have permission for the KMS key.

7️⃣ Scenario: You need to replicate objects between regions automatically. How will you configure it?
Answer:
Use Cross-Region Replication (CRR).
Requirements:
Enable Versioning on both source and destination buckets.
Set replication rule specifying destination bucket & region.
Attach IAM role with replication permissions.
Choose replication options: entire bucket or specific prefixes/tags.
S3 automatically replicates new objects (not old ones).

8️⃣ Scenario: You have millions of small files — S3 listing is slow. How do you optimize performance?
Answer:
Use prefix-based naming to spread load across partitions (e.g., images/2025/11/...).
Avoid sequential prefixes like file1, file2, file3.
Use S3 Inventory Reports for metadata instead of listing all objects.
Consider S3 Batch Operations for large-scale actions.

9️⃣ Scenario: You need to upload 100 GB files to S3 reliably. How can you do that efficiently?
Answer:
Use Multipart Upload:
Splits the file into parts and uploads them in parallel.
If any part fails, only that part is retried.
Speeds up large uploads and provides fault tolerance.
AWS CLI Example:
aws s3 cp largefile.zip s3://mybucket/ --storage-class STANDARD --expected-size 100G

🔟 Scenario: You need to ensure only encrypted data is stored in S3.
Answer:
Enable Bucket Default Encryption (SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS).
Use a Bucket Policy to enforce encryption:
{
  "Effect": "Deny",
  "Principal": "*",
  "Action": "s3:PutObject",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::mybucket/*",
  "Condition": {
    "StringNotEquals": { "s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption": "aws:kms" }
  }
}

11️⃣ Scenario: Your S3 bill is very high. How do you analyze and reduce cost?

Answer:
Enable S3 Storage Lens or Cost Explorer to view usage patterns.
Check for:
Old data that can move to Glacier or delete.
Unused old versions if versioning is enabled.
Enable Intelligent-Tiering for dynamic cost optimization.
Use Lifecycle Policies and S3 Object Lambda for efficient management.

12️⃣ Scenario: How do you make sure a user can only upload but not delete objects in S3?

Answer:
Attach an IAM Policy like:
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": ["s3:PutObject"],
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::mybucket/*"
},
{
  "Effect": "Deny",
  "Action": ["s3:DeleteObject"],
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::mybucket/*"
}

13️⃣ Scenario: How to serve private content (e.g., paid videos) from S3 securely?
Answer:
Use CloudFront Signed URLs or Signed Cookies.
S3 objects stay private; CloudFront validates user signature before allowing download.
Great for OTT or SaaS use cases.

14️⃣ Scenario: You want to log all access requests to S3 objects.
Answer:
Enable S3 Server Access Logging or CloudTrail Data Events.
Logs can be sent to another S3 bucket for analysis.
Example use: detect unauthorized access or compliance monitoring.

15️⃣ Scenario: Your S3 replication stopped working. What do you check?
Answer:
Ensure versioning is still enabled on both buckets.
Verify IAM replication role permissions.
Check replication configuration JSON for prefix/filter mismatches.

Look for KMS encryption issues — destination must allow replication role to use the key.
